{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR-ATTESTED — PAYLOAD PARITY, TOOL PARITY, and the capability differences. Captured\noutbound requests, compared field by field. Not a claim from reading code.\n\nMETHOD, and why it costs nothing. For ONE logical call — an agentic `code-quality` review\nRunRequest, identical object on both sides — the production path runs to the point where the\nprovider request is fully assembled, and is then aborted AT THE BOUNDARY:\n  * Anthropic arm: the real builder runs and wraps an `httpx.MockTransport` (the technique\n    `tests/unit/test_llm_provider_factory.py` already uses), so the genuine SDK assembles the\n    body; the handler records `request.content` and raises.\n  * Bedrock arm: the real boto3 `bedrock-runtime` client is constructed via the production\n    `build_bedrock_provider` path and only its `converse` is intercepted, recording the params\n    dict and raising.\nZERO network calls, ZERO tokens, on both arms. Captured at HEAD 170261318f.\n\n  Anthropic endpoint  https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages?beta=true\n  Bedrock  method     bedrock-runtime.Converse (us-east-1)\n\nRESULT — EQUIVALENT ON EVERY SEMANTIC FIELD, for BOTH class slots, with and without a requested\ntemperature. The only field that differs is the model id, which is the variable under test:\n\n  slot      request            system  messages  tools  max_tokens  temperature\n  standard  no temperature     SAME    SAME      SAME   16000/16000 none/none\n  standard  temperature=0.0    SAME    SAME      SAME   16000/16000 0.0/0.0\n  frontier  no temperature     SAME    SAME      SAME   16000/16000 none/none\n  frontier  temperature=0.0    SAME    SAME      SAME   16000/16000 none/none  (withdrawn, below)\n\n  system prompt   60 chars, byte-identical concatenated text on both providers\n  messages        1 user message, 2486 chars, role+text byte-identical on both providers\n  model ids       claude-sonnet-4-6 <-> us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n                  claude-opus-4-8   <-> us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\n\nTOOL PARITY — 6 tools on both providers, identical NAMES, identical DESCRIPTIONS, identical\nINPUT SCHEMAS (compared as sorted JSON):\n\n  comment_ticket, list_directory, read_file, resolve_symbol, search_files, show_ticket\n\n  Worked example of the same tool under each envelope:\n    Anthropic  {\"name\": \"read_file\", \"description\": \"Read a UTF-8 text file under the repo\n               root. ...\", \"input_schema\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\":\n               {\"path\": ..., \"line_start\": ..., \"line_end\": ...}, \"required\": [\"path\"], ...}}\n    Bedrock    {\"toolSpec\": {\"name\": \"read_file\", \"description\": <same>, \"inputSchema\":\n               {\"json\": <the same schema object>}}}\n\nENVELOPE DIFFERENCES — ENUMERATED, not waved at. These are the ONLY things the comparison\nnormalizes away; anything else would surface as a differing field:\n  1. model id: Anthropic `model` takes the BARE id; Bedrock `modelId` takes the INFERENCE-PROFILE\n     id (the `us.` form). A plain on-demand id is refused by Bedrock.\n  2. max tokens: Anthropic top-level `max_tokens`; Bedrock `inferenceConfig.maxTokens`.\n  3. sampling: Anthropic top-level `temperature`; Bedrock `inferenceConfig.temperature`.\n  4. system prompt: Anthropic a list of `{type: \"text\", text}` blocks; Bedrock a list of `{text}`\n     blocks. Same concatenated text.\n  5. prompt cache marker: Anthropic a `cache_control: {type: \"ephemeral\", ttl: \"5m\"}` ATTRIBUTE\n     on the block it terminates; Bedrock a standalone `{\"cachePoint\": {\"type\": \"default\"}}` LIST\n     ENTRY. Note Bedrock's form carries NO TTL — a real expressiveness difference, not just\n     spelling, though rebar sets no non-default TTL today.\n  6. tools: Anthropic `tools: [{name, description, input_schema}]`; Bedrock\n     `toolConfig.tools: [{toolSpec: {name, description, inputSchema: {json}}}]`, plus its own\n     trailing `{\"cachePoint\": {...}}` entry.\n  7. tool choice: Anthropic sends `tool_choice: {\"type\": \"auto\"}` explicitly; Converse omits the\n     field and defaults to auto.\n  8. streaming: Anthropic carries a `stream` field (false here); Bedrock selects a different\n     METHOD (`converse` vs `converse_stream`) instead.\n\nCAPABILITY DIFFERENCES THAT LEGITIMATELY ALTER THE PAYLOAD — one found, and it is EXPLICIT and\nSYMMETRIC. With `temperature=0.0` requested:\n    sonnet  anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6       -> temperature 0.0 SENT\n            bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 -> inferenceConfig.temperature 0.0 SENT\n    opus    anthropic:claude-opus-4-8         -> temperature WITHDRAWN (absent)\n            bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8   -> temperature WITHDRAWN (absent)\nThe withdrawal is driven by the MODEL's capability record\n(`capabilities._MODEL_ID_CAPABILITY_OVERRIDES`, `supports_temperature: False`), not by the\nprovider, so it applies to opus on BOTH providers and to neither sonnet arm. Withdrawing it\nchanges nothing else in the payload. This matches the live Step-1 probe, where Converse returned\n`ValidationException: ... \\`temperature\\` is deprecated for this model` for\n`us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8`, and explains why 90 frontier v2 case-runs produced ZERO\nsampling-parameter rejections. No OTHER capability difference altered either payload.\n\nREGRESSION-GATED, not just observed. The capture and the comparison are committed as offline\ntests in `tests/unit/test_provider_parity.py` (they make no network call and run in ~5s):\n`test_the_outbound_payload_is_equivalent_across_providers[standard|frontier]`,\n`test_the_tool_set_offered_to_the_model_is_identical_across_providers[standard|frontier]`,\n`test_the_temperature_capability_difference_is_symmetric_and_explicit`, and\n`test_the_enumerated_envelope_differences_are_the_only_ones_normalized`.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE that these are not vacuous: deleting the\n`\"us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8\": {\"supports_temperature\": False}` entry from\n`src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` — a real production-side regression that would make the\ncapability handling provider-ASYMMETRIC — turns the symmetry test RED with\n`assert (None is None and 0.0 is None)`: opus withdrew temperature on direct Anthropic while\nstill sending it to Bedrock. Restored, green. The payload tests also assert the captured call is\nnon-empty (60-char system, 2486-char message, 6 tools), so they cannot pass on an empty capture.\n\nCONCLUSION: PAYLOAD PARITY and TOOL PARITY both HOLD across both class slots. No remediation is\nrequired under this criterion, and no undocumented capability difference was found.\n"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785725563295318001,"uuid":"d60fdca1-c69f-462a-9551-293b6cc77cc8"}